        "like": 1
      },
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "To add some more context.\n\nAs of May 31st 2023, Ripperstore no longer offers the option to \"Blacklist\" your account from their \"service\"\n\nHowever, if you managed to be blacklisted before then it's still valid.\n\nNote that Blacklisting does not mean that your avatars are prevented from being uploaded to the site or viewed publicly, it just means that they can't be \"purchased\" with the credits they award you for using their mod to upload avatars. Thankfully EAC has made it much more difficult for users to earn the credits (on pc. There's a mod for quest that still works apparently) but not impossible as they now have an option for users to watch ads to earn credits, which means that not only are they providing a service that directly violates VRChat's TOU and copyright policy, they're also indirectly monetizing it.\n\nRipperstore also offers a services to directly re-upload avatars, RIP files from .vrca files, and a tool to acquire .vrca using an avatar's ID; All of which violate VRChat's TOU.\n\nEspecially now that monetization for avatars and worlds is coming, I would love an update on VRChat's stance on this. Thank you.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-07-27T06:42:57.722Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Rippers are nothing new to VRchat and are definitely a consistent problem in the community, and while the efforts of keeping avatar/world rippers out of the game have been pretty strong, there may need to be some buckling down on promotion for the act outside the game itself. Whether this is the place to post it or not, if VRchat would be willing to look into it, there is a site, <link snipped>, which shows to have people's avatars on the site, and shows that you can purchase private ones with credits earned by submitting avatars. It also does advertise a mod which recent updates should easily take care of, but the site itself genuinely encourages taking people's creations under the guise of \"preservation,\" which may sound like justifiable reasoning but looks suspicious more than anything. They say you can have your avatars \"blacklisted\" from the site by reaching out to their discord, but they can take days to reply and refuse to delete your content without display of a copyright license. \nI need a license to preserve my PERSONAL avatar? Are you serious?\nVery fishy third party service. 0/10. Take down pls.",
